Comments:
Evaluated by Caleb Marshall 2025
1) Observed resonances adopted from Schmalbrock 1983, Highland and Thwaites 1968, and Smulders 1983. All values normalized to Er=1365 keV resonance, the value of
which was adjusted from the value reported in Schmalbrock 1983 Table 3. The adjustment factored in the correction to the erroneous 20Ne(p, g) resonance strength value
used to normalize one of the values. We also include the unadjusted value reported in Smulders in the average. The claims by Schmalbrock that Smulders stopping powers
are in the lab frame are in error, since the latter study did not use stopping powers and relied instead on the integrated yield. Due to the mild tension between
Smulders and Schmalbrock the expectation value method was used for the suggested value of the Er=1365 keV resonance.
2) Unobserved resonances are based on the relative spectroscopic factors reported in Anantaraman 1976. Several strong alpha cluster states go unreported in that study,
but further information is unavailable. The absolute scale was established from the ratio of ground state spectroscopic factors given in Anantaraman using the ground
state 20Ne spectroscopic factors reported in Carey 1984 and Mao 1996 along with the absolute value reported in Mao. These averages yield 0.68.
3) Spin, parity, and energy assignments were adopted from Basunia 2022. No attempt was made to be consistent with the 23Na(p, g)/(p, a) evaluation.
4) Upper limits come from either: Schmalbrock 1983 or twice the Wigner limit.
5) No matching necessary up to 10 GK.